WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:02.820
Face it: Star Wars shouldn't have worked.

00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:08.240
It was a science fiction fairy tale made when
Hollywood was cranking out gritty, character-driven

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:08.740
dramas.

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:12.780
It starred a handful of unknown actors and
a man in a fur suit.

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:16.620
It begins with 20 minutes of robots wandering
through a desert.

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:20.100
The main villain of the original trilogy is
a space wizard.

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:24.880
That sounds like a disaster, not the beginning
of a multi-billion dollar franchise.

00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:30.800
Yet, despite all odds, Star Wars didn't just
clean up at the box office, it literally reinvented

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:32.540
the entire Hollywood machine.

00:00:32.740 --> 00:00:35.360
And it came so close to never happening.

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:36.120
Here's why.

00:00:36.740 --> 00:00:37.900
Flash of genius

00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:42.030
Star Wars didn't spring fully-formed from
George Lucas' head.

00:00:42.030 --> 00:00:46.790
In fact, if Lucas had his way, he would've
made an entirely different movie.

00:00:46.790 --> 00:00:49.900
As a child, Lucas loved the old Flash Gordon
serials

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:53.340
"I wonder what happened to Dale and Dr. Zarkov?"

00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:56.320
"No doubt my father is holding them prisoners."

00:00:58.060 --> 00:01:04.540
After Lucas' debut, the cold and lifeless
science fiction thriller THX 1138, failed

00:01:04.550 --> 00:01:10.200
to excite audiences and critics, Lucas wanted
to make something with more heart, so he sought

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:12.460
the rights to make a new Flash Gordon film.

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:17.570
But Lucas ultimately couldn't seal the deal
with King Features Syndicate, which published

00:01:17.570 --> 00:01:22.770
Alex Raymond's original comic strip and owned
the rights to the character — so, fortunately

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:27.340
for Star Wars fans, he decided to invent his
own Flash Gordon-like saga.

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:29.240
"Prepare my rocket fleet at once.

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:33.920
We will go to the sky city of Vultan and teach
him a needed lesson."

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:36.440
Studio resistance

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:44.060
While THX 1138 was a flop, Lucas' second feature,
American Graffiti, was one of the most profitable

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:47.120
movies of all time, and a Best Picture nominee
...

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:50.020
"How'd you like to ride around with me for
awhile?"

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:51.160
"I'm sorry, I can't.

00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:52.300
I'm going steady."

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:57.580
Universal Pictures executives were delighted
at the film's success, and wanted more.

00:01:57.860 --> 00:02:02.980
Lucas, however, had his heart set on Star
Wars and submitted a 12-page outline for the

00:02:02.980 --> 00:02:03.480
flick.

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:08.979
The studio passed, and many of Lucas' friends
and colleagues agreed, encouraging Lucas to

00:02:08.980 --> 00:02:12.220
make a "deeper" film, more grounded and serious.

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:13.900
But Lucas wouldn't listen.

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:15.840
Sci-fi dry spell

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:19.440
Universal isn't the only studio that passed
on Star Wars.

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:25.360
United Artists and Disney said no, too, arguing
that science-fiction wasn't popular and wouldn't

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:25.860
sell.

00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:31.980
After all, as Star Wars producer Gary Kurtz
noted to IGN, it took seven years for 1968's

00:02:31.990 --> 00:02:39.450
2001: A Space Odyssey to turn a profit, and
the last big space opera, 1956's Forbidden

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:43.900
Planet, was almost 20 years old when Lucas
was pitching Star Wars.

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:47.460
"Why you low-living contraption!

00:02:47.460 --> 00:02:48.960
I ought to take a can opener to you!"

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:50.800
"Quiet, please.

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:52.160
I am analyzing."

00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:58.540
When Lucas tried to explain to the studios
that Star Wars is more fantasy than science

00:02:58.550 --> 00:03:04.950
fiction, the debate was lost on 20th Century
Fox president Alan Ladd, Jr., who just wanted

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:05.950
Lucas, period.

00:03:05.950 --> 00:03:09.730
"He said, 'I don't understand this, but I
loved American Graffiti, and whatever you

00:03:09.730 --> 00:03:10.660
do is okay with me.'"

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:17.300
So in 1973, Lucas finally inked a deal to
write and direct Star Wars for Fox.

00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:18.940
Financial woes

00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:23.480
Here's one of Star Wars' biggest secrets:
it was not an expensive film.

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:28.480
While Star Wars established the template for
the expensive Hollywood blockbuster, its initial

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:30.140
budget was relatively tiny.

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:36.040
Fox gave Lucas and his crew just $8 million,
but unfortunately, that wasn't quite enough.

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:41.400
When he asked for more, Fox shut down pre-production
and forced the team to come up with a new

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:44.440
budget, which came out to about $10 million.

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:49.880
Lucas was forced to scrap a number of scenes,
including Han Solo's confrontation with Jabba

00:03:49.880 --> 00:03:55.020
the Hutt, later controversially added via
CGI to the Special Edition of the film.

00:03:56.080 --> 00:03:57.140
Trouble in Tunisia

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:03.260
Tatooine, Luke Skywalker's home planet, was
originally supposed to be a jungle, but Lucas

00:04:03.260 --> 00:04:08.420
changed it to a desert planet after a location
scout in the Philippines irritated his skin.

00:04:08.420 --> 00:04:13.050
While Tatooine's twin suns setting over the
dunes is one of the most iconic images in

00:04:13.050 --> 00:04:18.470
a franchise full of iconic images, Tunisia
isn't the most hospitable environment for

00:04:18.470 --> 00:04:21.650
filmmaking, and the production fell behind
immediately.

00:04:21.650 --> 00:04:26.820
Allegedly, the sand didn't mix well with the
cameras, damaging lots of expensive equipment.

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:33.259
Windstorms ravaged the sets, and R2-D2's controls
didn't work particularly well, either, as

00:04:33.259 --> 00:04:36.430
the sand interfered with the remote's radio
signals.

00:04:36.430 --> 00:04:37.540
And then there was the weather.

00:04:37.830 --> 00:04:42.500
On the second day of shooting, a freak rainstorm
— the first winter rain that the area had

00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:46.400
gotten in 50 years — made filming practically
impossible.

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:48.699
Delays almost spelled doom

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:53.800
Thanks to the problems in Tunisia, Star Wars
was behind schedule by the time that the crew

00:04:53.800 --> 00:04:54.740
moved to England.

00:04:55.100 --> 00:04:57.300
Unfortunately, things didn't get much better.

00:04:57.580 --> 00:05:02.699
Union rules severely limited when the cast
and crew could work, and it didn't help that

00:05:02.699 --> 00:05:06.979
Lucas was still tweaking the script while
filming, or that Lucas had to fire the film's

00:05:06.980 --> 00:05:11.120
original editor, meaning an hour's worth of
footage had to be recut.

00:05:11.340 --> 00:05:13.160
Filming began in March 1976.

00:05:13.240 --> 00:05:17.600
By July, Lucas was 15 days behind schedule.

00:05:17.610 --> 00:05:22.819
Fox issued an ultimatum: Lucas had to finish
the film in a week, or the studio was going

00:05:22.820 --> 00:05:23.720
to pull the plug.

00:05:24.420 --> 00:05:26.080
The studio almost buried it

00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:31.960
While an early screening for studio executives
went well, Fox wasn't sure how to sell Star

00:05:31.960 --> 00:05:33.980
Wars to the public — so, they didn't.

00:05:34.280 --> 00:05:39.260
Instead, the studio put its marketing muscle
behind The Other Side of Midnight, which was

00:05:39.260 --> 00:05:42.520
based on a best-selling novel, and had a built-in
audience.

00:05:42.820 --> 00:05:47.069
Meanwhile, theater owners thought that Star
Wars was a "kiddie movie" and didn't want

00:05:47.069 --> 00:05:48.069
to screen it.

00:05:48.069 --> 00:05:52.629
So in order to boost circulation, Fox forced
theater chains that wanted The Other Side

00:05:52.629 --> 00:05:54.949
of Midnight to book Star Wars as well.

00:05:54.949 --> 00:06:00.030
As Star Wars broke record after record for
both attendance and box office returns, Fox

00:06:00.030 --> 00:06:02.800
expanded into as many markets as possible.

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:08.949
By September, Star Wars was in 1,100 theaters
— an unheard of number at the time — and

00:06:08.949 --> 00:06:12.039
its first theatrical run lasted for over a
year.

00:06:12.039 --> 00:06:17.659
By then, it was clear: despite the odds, Star
Wars was a hit, and Hollywood would never

00:06:17.660 --> 00:06:18.320
be the same.

00:06:18.800 --> 00:06:19.680
Thanks for watching!

00:06:19.940 --> 00:06:22.900
